    {
      "id": "2GF3H32whJWKKvcCj2XcN",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -55958.08953693962,
      "y": -6195.593574807742,
      "width": 1886.3787841796875,
      "height": 300,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a6",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1454642904,
      "version": 1538,
      "versionNonce": 1007296472,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784526308671,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure we're fault tolerant and don't loose progress?\n- Currently our crawler service is doing a lot, hitting DNS, fetching, extracting, parsing, adding new urls and after politeness and efficiency it will become more complex\n- Not ideal from a fault tolerant perspective, if any single task fails, the entire processing for the URL fails\n- Fetching web pages is the most likely task to fail, maybe server is slow, connection slow, page too large etc. this should be separated\n- Allows us to retry, scale individual segments independently\n- The two components now are : \n    - Crawler : Fetches the HTML and stores it in S3 and URL metadata in DynamoDB\n    - Parser : Fetches that HTML using S3 link, parse and extract and then push urls to frontier queue and parsed data back to S3\n- Pipelining allows you to isolate failures to a single stage and retry that stage without losing progress on the rest of the data.\n- URL Metadata(DynamoDB) stores the URL that have been fetched and processed, stores the link to S3 where HTML is stored and also the S3 link where extracted text is stored.\n- Its an anti pattern to store raw HTML is queue itself, queues are not optimised for large payloads, just store {id, s3link} in queue.\n- Separating intends also helps to quickly modify parsing process, say tomorrow ML team requests image OCR extraction as well we can easily enhance the parser and crawler remains unaffected.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure we're fault tolerant and don't loose progress?\n- Currently our crawler service is doing a lot, hitting DNS, fetching, extracting, parsing, adding new urls and after politeness and efficiency it will become more complex\n- Not ideal from a fault tolerant perspective, if any single task fails, the entire processing for the URL fails\n- Fetching web pages is the most likely task to fail, maybe server is slow, connection slow, page too large etc. this should be separated\n- Allows us to retry, scale individual segments independently\n- The two components now are : \n    - Crawler : Fetches the HTML and stores it in S3 and URL metadata in DynamoDB\n    - Parser : Fetches that HTML using S3 link, parse and extract and then push urls to frontier queue and parsed data back to S3\n- Pipelining allows you to isolate failures to a single stage and retry that stage without losing progress on the rest of the data.\n- URL Metadata(DynamoDB) stores the URL that have been fetched and processed, stores the link to S3 where HTML is stored and also the S3 link where extracted text is stored.\n- Its an anti pattern to store raw HTML is queue itself, queues are not optimised for large payloads, just store {id, s3link} in queue.\n- Separating intends also helps to quickly modify parsing process, say tomorrow ML team requests image OCR extraction as well we can easily enhance the parser and crawler remains unaffected.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "8FrTBZdvGDHX5vWUr696L",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -55944.73412080778,
      "y": -5818.376981117928,
      "width": 2709.218017578125,
      "height": 500,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a7",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 738660056,
      "version": 1224,
      "versionNonce": 1828508584,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784527001613,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What if we fail to fetch a URL?\n- Many website may no longer exist, or experiencing downtime, we'll want to retry on failures\n\n1. Bad Solution : In Memory Timer\n- Wait a few seconds in memory and try again\nProblem : If crawler goes down we loose the timer, very likely the fetch won't succeed after few seconds we might need to wait longer\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Kafka with Manual Exponential Backoff\n- Kafka doesn't support retries out of the box, need to implement it ourselves \n- Have a separate topic for failed URLs and a separate service to read from this topic and retries fetch with exponential backoff\n- How much to backoff? store the time that the next fetch should occur in message itself, when consumer reads it, checks the time and if its in future it waits until that time to retry fetch.\nProblem : Complex to implement and maintain.\n\n3. Great Solution : SQS with Exponential Backoff\n- SQS doesn't have inbuilt exponential backoff setting, but provides primitives to build one easily.\n- Its Visibility Timeout : when a consumer receives a message but doesn't delete it, the message becomes visible again after the visibility timeout expires (default 30 seconds, configurable up to 12 hours).\n- To implement exponential backoff, we use the ChangeMessageVisibility API to dynamically adjust the timeout based on the ApproximateReceiveCount attribute, which tracks how many times a message has been received.\n- after each failed fetch, we calculate the next visibility timeout as and call ChangeMessageVisibility to set it. This keeps the message hidden until we're ready to retry.\nProblem : We don't want to retry indefinitely. After a certain number of failures (configured via the queue's redrive policy using maxReceiveCount), the message is automatically moved to a dead-letter queue (DLQ). For our purposes, we'll consider the site offline after 5 retries.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What if we fail to fetch a URL?\n- Many website may no longer exist, or experiencing downtime, we'll want to retry on failures\n\n1. Bad Solution : In Memory Timer\n- Wait a few seconds in memory and try again\nProblem : If crawler goes down we loose the timer, very likely the fetch won't succeed after few seconds we might need to wait longer\n\n\n2. Good Solution : Kafka with Manual Exponential Backoff\n- Kafka doesn't support retries out of the box, need to implement it ourselves \n- Have a separate topic for failed URLs and a separate service to read from this topic and retries fetch with exponential backoff\n- How much to backoff? store the time that the next fetch should occur in message itself, when consumer reads it, checks the time and if its in future it waits until that time to retry fetch.\nProblem : Complex to implement and maintain.\n\n3. Great Solution : SQS with Exponential Backoff\n- SQS doesn't have inbuilt exponential backoff setting, but provides primitives to build one easily.\n- Its Visibility Timeout : when a consumer receives a message but doesn't delete it, the message becomes visible again after the visibility timeout expires (default 30 seconds, configurable up to 12 hours).\n- To implement exponential backoff, we use the ChangeMessageVisibility API to dynamically adjust the timeout based on the ApproximateReceiveCount attribute, which tracks how many times a message has been received.\n- after each failed fetch, we calculate the next visibility timeout as and call ChangeMessageVisibility to set it. This keeps the message hidden until we're ready to retry.\nProblem : We don't want to retry indefinitely. After a certain number of failures (configured via the queue's redrive policy using maxReceiveCount), the message is automatically moved to a dead-letter queue (DLQ). For our purposes, we'll consider the site offline after 5 retries.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "QuxCNplusTuu_G2fQAqCu",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -55936.63244061164,
      "y": -5230.923697487939,
      "width": 1855.71875,
      "height": 325,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 536111576,
      "version": 605,
      "versionNonce": 527825064,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784527404266,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "What happens if a crawler goes down?\n- We start a new one but gracefully handle the URL that was being processed at that instant\n- URL stays in the queue until its confirmed by the crawler that its been e2e processed. This way that URL will be picked up by another Crawler\n- Apache Kafka : \n    - Kafka retains messages in a log and does not remove them even after they are read.\n    - Crawlers track their progress via offsets, which are not updated in Kafka until the URL is successfully fetched and processed.\n    - Deletes it after the expiry period default is 7 days\n- Amazon's SQS : \n    - With SQS, messages remain in the queue until they are explicitly deleted.\n    - A visibility timeout hides a message from other crawlers once it's fetched.\n    - If the crawler fails before confirming successful processing, the message will automatically become visible again after the timeout expires, allowing another crawler to attempt the fetch.\n    - Once e2e processed crawler delete the message from SQS\n- Same applies to the Parsing Queue",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "What happens if a crawler goes down?\n- We start a new one but gracefully handle the URL that was being processed at that instant\n- URL stays in the queue until its confirmed by the crawler that its been e2e processed. This way that URL will be picked up by another Crawler\n- Apache Kafka : \n    - Kafka retains messages in a log and does not remove them even after they are read.\n    - Crawlers track their progress via offsets, which are not updated in Kafka until the URL is successfully fetched and processed.\n    - Deletes it after the expiry period default is 7 days\n- Amazon's SQS : \n    - With SQS, messages remain in the queue until they are explicitly deleted.\n    - A visibility timeout hides a message from other crawlers once it's fetched.\n    - If the crawler fails before confirming successful processing, the message will automatically become visible again after the timeout expires, allowing another crawler to attempt the fetch.\n    - Once e2e processed crawler delete the message from SQS\n- Same applies to the Parsing Queue",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "Mx4vreFQZ0t9D4-hNvxIH",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -55929.25040686874,
      "y": -4789.622008949333,
      "width": 2460.598388671875,
      "height": 600,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a9",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 798611880,
      "version": 1391,
      "versionNonce": 1251764696,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784528808152,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to ensure politeness and adhere to robots.txt?\n- Politeness refers to being respectful with the resources of the websites we are crawling.\n- This involves ensuring that our crawling activity does not disrupt the normal function of the site by overloading its servers, respecting the website's bandwidth, and adhering to any specific restrictions or rules set by the site administrators.\n- robots.txt is a file that websites use to communicate with web crawlers. It tells crawlers which pages they are allowed to crawl, how frequently they can crawl.\n        User-agent: * \n        Disallow: /private/\n        Crawl-delay: 10\n- * means applies to all crawlers, Disallow specifies what domain(/private/) is not allowed to crawl, crawl-delay is how much time to wait b/w requests.\n- Crawl-delay is not part of the official Robots Exclusion Protocol standard and is ignored by Googlebot, but our design should respect it.\n- To do this, we need to:\n    - Before crawling check robots.txt, if we're allowed, if not skip it, also honour crawl-delay\n    - Rate limit the requests made to any single domain, Industry standard is 1 req/sec\n- First download the robots.txt load it in Crawler, check if allowed? if not remove from queue and don't process else process and remove from the queue.\n- To implement crawl delay add lastCrawlTime to URL Metadata Table to store the last time we crawled each domain, and accordingly use SQS's ChangeMessageVisibility to extend visibility timeout.\n- To avoid two crawlers pull out the URLs from the same domain simultaneously and see a stale lastCrawlTime, to prevent this user atomic operation like Redis(SET & NX and TTL to match the crawl delay) to acquire a per-domain lock before crawling.\n- DelaySeconds only applies when sending a new message. For messages already in-flight (which is our case), use ChangeMessageVisibility to defer reprocessing. Visibility timeout can be extended up to 12 hours per message.\n- So all steps are : \n    - Fetch the robots.txt file for the domain.\n    - Parse the robots.txt file and store it in the Metadata DB.\n    - When we pull a URL off the queue, check the rules stored in the Metadata DB for that domain.\n    - If the URL is disallowed, ack the message and move on to the next URL.\n    - If the URL is allowed, check the Crawl-delay directive.\n    - If the Crawl-delay time has not passed since the last crawl, use ChangeMessageVisibility to extend the visibility timeout and defer reprocessing.\n    - If the Crawl-delay time has passed, crawl the page and update the last crawl time for the domain.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to ensure politeness and adhere to robots.txt?\n- Politeness refers to being respectful with the resources of the websites we are crawling.\n- This involves ensuring that our crawling activity does not disrupt the normal function of the site by overloading its servers, respecting the website's bandwidth, and adhering to any specific restrictions or rules set by the site administrators.\n- robots.txt is a file that websites use to communicate with web crawlers. It tells crawlers which pages they are allowed to crawl, how frequently they can crawl.\n        User-agent: * \n        Disallow: /private/\n        Crawl-delay: 10\n- * means applies to all crawlers, Disallow specifies what domain(/private/) is not allowed to crawl, crawl-delay is how much time to wait b/w requests.\n- Crawl-delay is not part of the official Robots Exclusion Protocol standard and is ignored by Googlebot, but our design should respect it.\n- To do this, we need to:\n    - Before crawling check robots.txt, if we're allowed, if not skip it, also honour crawl-delay\n    - Rate limit the requests made to any single domain, Industry standard is 1 req/sec\n- First download the robots.txt load it in Crawler, check if allowed? if not remove from queue and don't process else process and remove from the queue.\n- To implement crawl delay add lastCrawlTime to URL Metadata Table to store the last time we crawled each domain, and accordingly use SQS's ChangeMessageVisibility to extend visibility timeout.\n- To avoid two crawlers pull out the URLs from the same domain simultaneously and see a stale lastCrawlTime, to prevent this user atomic operation like Redis(SET & NX and TTL to match the crawl delay) to acquire a per-domain lock before crawling.\n- DelaySeconds only applies when sending a new message. For messages already in-flight (which is our case), use ChangeMessageVisibility to defer reprocessing. Visibility timeout can be extended up to 12 hours per message.\n- So all steps are : \n    - Fetch the robots.txt file for the domain.\n    - Parse the robots.txt file and store it in the Metadata DB.\n    - When we pull a URL off the queue, check the rules stored in the Metadata DB for that domain.\n    - If the URL is disallowed, ack the message and move on to the next URL.\n    - If the URL is allowed, check the Crawl-delay directive.\n    - If the Crawl-delay time has not passed since the last crawl, use ChangeMessageVisibility to extend the visibility timeout and defer reprocessing.\n    - If the Crawl-delay time has passed, crawl the page and update the last crawl time for the domain.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "kdjHiDTyhMwRqPpjccmmA",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -55909.83353086802,
      "y": -4128.153766521862,
      "width": 2076.5185546875,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aA",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1222574248,
      "version": 875,
      "versionNonce": 1152421800,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784529268732,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to rate limit?\n- Rate limiting gets trickier when multiple crawlers try to hit the same domain\n- Use Redis to implement a global, domain-specific rate limiter to track req per domain per sec\n- Each crawler before making a request checks Redis to ensure its not rate limited, use sliding window algo\n- Issue is risk of synchronized behaviour among multiple crawlers, i.e. if several crawlers are waiting to make req and simultaneously retry when window resets they all try and only one succeeds and process repeats\n- This could be solved using Jitter, i.e. introduce a small amt of randomness to rate-limit-algo, to prevent this behaviour\n- Add a random delay to each crawler's request ensuring they all don't retry at the same time.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to rate limit?\n- Rate limiting gets trickier when multiple crawlers try to hit the same domain\n- Use Redis to implement a global, domain-specific rate limiter to track req per domain per sec\n- Each crawler before making a request checks Redis to ensure its not rate limited, use sliding window algo\n- Issue is risk of synchronized behaviour among multiple crawlers, i.e. if several crawlers are waiting to make req and simultaneously retry when window resets they all try and only one succeeds and process repeats\n- This could be solved using Jitter, i.e. introduce a small amt of randomness to rate-limit-algo, to prevent this behaviour\n- Add a random delay to each crawler's request ensuring they all don't retry at the same time.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "efu_58i8Dz6B-VDT0d0fi",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -55895.92941826322,
      "y": -3844.42749475902,
      "width": 2275.89794921875,
      "height": 225,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aB",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1512558760,
      "version": 1202,
      "versionNonce": 503650728,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784533138126,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "How to scale to 10B pages and crawl them under 5 days?\n- Note : save scaling discussion until the end as you have all optimised pieces in place by this time.\n- Our single server won't be able to handle this load as I/O intensive task, with avg page size of 2MB\n- AWS's network optimised instance handles 200Gbps => 200 Gbps/ 8 bits/byte / 2MB/page ~ 12500 pages/sec\n- But we can't use entire bandwidth to do this as there are factors limiting us i.e. server response delay, DNS resolution, rate limiting, politeness etc.\n- We say we're only able to utilize 30% of the bandwidth 12500 pages/sec * 30% = 3750 pages/sec\n- Might wonder we've to crawl so many pages with 1req/sec rate limit but the insight is we're crawling millions of different domains in parallel. Each crawler has 1000s of concurrent connections while single domain seem atmost 1req/sec\n- 10,000,000,000 pages / 3,750 pages/second ≈ 2,666,667 seconds ≈ 30.9 days for a single machine 30.9 days / 8 machines ≈ 3.9 days, meets our 5 day requirement\n- There's a lot of estimations here but in reality you need to do a load testing to get to a more accurate number",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "How to scale to 10B pages and crawl them under 5 days?\n- Note : save scaling discussion until the end as you have all optimised pieces in place by this time.\n- Our single server won't be able to handle this load as I/O intensive task, with avg page size of 2MB\n- AWS's network optimised instance handles 200Gbps => 200 Gbps/ 8 bits/byte / 2MB/page ~ 12500 pages/sec\n- But we can't use entire bandwidth to do this as there are factors limiting us i.e. server response delay, DNS resolution, rate limiting, politeness etc.\n- We say we're only able to utilize 30% of the bandwidth 12500 pages/sec * 30% = 3750 pages/sec\n- Might wonder we've to crawl so many pages with 1req/sec rate limit but the insight is we're crawling millions of different domains in parallel. Each crawler has 1000s of concurrent connections while single domain seem atmost 1req/sec\n- 10,000,000,000 pages / 3,750 pages/second ≈ 2,666,667 seconds ≈ 30.9 days for a single machine 30.9 days / 8 machines ≈ 3.9 days, meets our 5 day requirement\n- There's a lot of estimations here but in reality you need to do a load testing to get to a more accurate number",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
