    {
      "id": "hDQUVBTn4M_dFZYXllRca",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -55891.29471406164,
      "y": -3429.472108131993,
      "width": 1653.3988037109375,
      "height": 175,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aF",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1085727656,
      "version": 836,
      "versionNonce": 853959848,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784533582740,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "DNS\n- DNS is also a potential bottleneck, tools like curl handles DNS automatically but issue happens at scale\n- 1000s of req/sec across millions of unique domains, DNS resolution struggles, each lookup for a new domain requires multiple network round-trips through DNS heirarchy\n- Mercator Crawler Paper found DNS lookup accounted for 70% of each thread's elapsed time before they built a custom resolver.\n- We're using 3rd party DNS resolver, and they have rate limiting but can be increased with more money\n- DNS Caching : first look from DNS then cache that domain and future lookups are from our local cache\n- Multiple DNS Providers : round-robin b/w multiple DNS providers reducing risk of rate limiting.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "DNS\n- DNS is also a potential bottleneck, tools like curl handles DNS automatically but issue happens at scale\n- 1000s of req/sec across millions of unique domains, DNS resolution struggles, each lookup for a new domain requires multiple network round-trips through DNS heirarchy\n- Mercator Crawler Paper found DNS lookup accounted for 70% of each thread's elapsed time before they built a custom resolver.\n- We're using 3rd party DNS resolver, and they have rate limiting but can be increased with more money\n- DNS Caching : first look from DNS then cache that domain and future lookups are from our local cache\n- Multiple DNS Providers : round-robin b/w multiple DNS providers reducing risk of rate limiting.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "7vtrnM60WYVgENJLH1YqL",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -55884.20234933127,
      "y": -3133.7336245029264,
      "width": 2651.818359375,
      "height": 1000,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "aG",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1734004904,
      "version": 3674,
      "versionNonce": 918363864,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1784539914810,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "how to make it more efficient?\n- Need to make sure we don't crawl pages that have already been crawled, check URL Metadata if its already been crawled before putting it on the queue, if it exists in our table skip it. This is URL Level Deduplication\n- But this isn't enough, different URLs can serve identical content For example, http://example.com and http://www.example.com might return the same page., Even completely different domain may have exactly identical content we need content-level deduplication \n- we hash the page content after fetching it and compare it with the hash we've seen before, if already stored this exact content we skip its parsing\n\n\n1. Great Solution : Hash and Store in Metadata DB with Index\n- Hash the content of the pages and store it in URL table, when we fetch a new URL, we hash its content and compare it to the hashes in MetadataDB\n- If match found, we skip the page, Need to build index on Hash Column, could be GSI in dynamoDB\nProblems : Index becomes large to search over time but could be overcome with modern DBs\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Bloom Filter\n- use a Bloom filter to store the hashes of the content of the pages we have already crawled.\n- for new pages we hash them and check the Bloom Filter, if the hash is not in Bloom, we definitely know we haven't crawled it before, if its in Bloom Filter we've probably crawled it(a small chance of false positive) and we skip it\n- Use Redis with the RedisBloom module to store the bloom filter. Use BF.ADD and BF.EXISTS\n- Or you could implement a Bloom filter using Redis bit operations or store it in application memory.\nProblem : False positives, we can argue that its acceptable tradeoff for performance benefits can reduce false positives by increasing the size of filter and number of hash functions used.\nBloom filter is bit of an OverKill here, but Index approach is more practical.\n\n\nhow to prevent from Crawler Traps?\n- Crawler traps are pages designed to keep crawlers on the site indefinitely. Can be created by having a page that links to itself many times or many other pages many times.\n- Implement maximum depths for our crawlers, i.e. the number of hops from a seed URL\n- Seed URL starts at depts 0, add a depth field to URL Metadata table and update this on each followed link from a page, and if reaches a certain threshold say 20, we stop crawling that branch.\n\n\n\n\nAdditional Deepdives\n- How to handle dynamic content? - Many websites are built with JS frameworks like React & Angular, this means the content of the page is not in the HTML that is returned by the server, but instead\n  loaded dynamically by JS. To handle this, we'll need to use a headless browser like Puppeteer to render the page and extract the content.\n- How to monitor the health of the system? - We can use a monitoring service like Datadog or New Relic to monitor the performance of the crawlers and parser workers and to alert us if anything goes wrong.\n- How to handle large files? - Some websites have very large files that we may not want to download. We can send an HTTP HEAD request first to check the Content-Length header and skip files that exceed a threshold (say 10MB) without downloading the full content.\n- How to handle continual updates? - We might need to retrain a model or our crawl is for a search engine that needs to be updated regularly, so need to run the crawler multiple times\n      - Add a new component \"URL Scheduler\" responsible for scheduling URLs to be crawled\n      - So rather than putting URLs on the queue directly from the parser workers, the parser workers would put URLs in the Metadata DB\n      - URL Scheduler would be responsible for scheduling URLs to be crawled by using some logic based on last crawl time, popularity, etc.\n- How to implement priority crawling? - If you need to prioritise certain URLs(eg. popular domains first), we use multiple SQS queues with different priorities and have crawlers poll high-priority queue first.\n    Kafka doesn't natively support priority-based consumption, but you can achieve a similar effect with separate topics per priority level.",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "how to make it more efficient?\n- Need to make sure we don't crawl pages that have already been crawled, check URL Metadata if its already been crawled before putting it on the queue, if it exists in our table skip it. This is URL Level Deduplication\n- But this isn't enough, different URLs can serve identical content For example, http://example.com and http://www.example.com might return the same page., Even completely different domain may have exactly identical content we need content-level deduplication \n- we hash the page content after fetching it and compare it with the hash we've seen before, if already stored this exact content we skip its parsing\n\n\n1. Great Solution : Hash and Store in Metadata DB with Index\n- Hash the content of the pages and store it in URL table, when we fetch a new URL, we hash its content and compare it to the hashes in MetadataDB\n- If match found, we skip the page, Need to build index on Hash Column, could be GSI in dynamoDB\nProblems : Index becomes large to search over time but could be overcome with modern DBs\n\n\n2. Great Solution : Bloom Filter\n- use a Bloom filter to store the hashes of the content of the pages we have already crawled.\n- for new pages we hash them and check the Bloom Filter, if the hash is not in Bloom, we definitely know we haven't crawled it before, if its in Bloom Filter we've probably crawled it(a small chance of false positive) and we skip it\n- Use Redis with the RedisBloom module to store the bloom filter. Use BF.ADD and BF.EXISTS\n- Or you could implement a Bloom filter using Redis bit operations or store it in application memory.\nProblem : False positives, we can argue that its acceptable tradeoff for performance benefits can reduce false positives by increasing the size of filter and number of hash functions used.\nBloom filter is bit of an OverKill here, but Index approach is more practical.\n\n\nhow to prevent from Crawler Traps?\n- Crawler traps are pages designed to keep crawlers on the site indefinitely. Can be created by having a page that links to itself many times or many other pages many times.\n- Implement maximum depths for our crawlers, i.e. the number of hops from a seed URL\n- Seed URL starts at depts 0, add a depth field to URL Metadata table and update this on each followed link from a page, and if reaches a certain threshold say 20, we stop crawling that branch.\n\n\n\n\nAdditional Deepdives\n- How to handle dynamic content? - Many websites are built with JS frameworks like React & Angular, this means the content of the page is not in the HTML that is returned by the server, but instead\n  loaded dynamically by JS. To handle this, we'll need to use a headless browser like Puppeteer to render the page and extract the content.\n- How to monitor the health of the system? - We can use a monitoring service like Datadog or New Relic to monitor the performance of the crawlers and parser workers and to alert us if anything goes wrong.\n- How to handle large files? - Some websites have very large files that we may not want to download. We can send an HTTP HEAD request first to check the Content-Length header and skip files that exceed a threshold (say 10MB) without downloading the full content.\n- How to handle continual updates? - We might need to retrain a model or our crawl is for a search engine that needs to be updated regularly, so need to run the crawler multiple times\n      - Add a new component \"URL Scheduler\" responsible for scheduling URLs to be crawled\n      - So rather than putting URLs on the queue directly from the parser workers, the parser workers would put URLs in the Metadata DB\n      - URL Scheduler would be responsible for scheduling URLs to be crawled by using some logic based on last crawl time, popularity, etc.\n- How to implement priority crawling? - If you need to prioritise certain URLs(eg. popular domains first), we use multiple SQS queues with different priorities and have crawlers poll high-priority queue first.\n    Kafka doesn't natively support priority-based consumption, but you can achieve a similar effect with separate topics per priority level.",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
